│  # Predictive Lift Maintenance Remains an Additive Expense Until            │
│  Regulations Permit Remote Sign-Off                                         │
│                                                                             │
│  **Summary** - Predictive maintenance cannot replace routine commercial     │
│  lift servicing because statutory safety regulations and insurance          │
│  policies mandate recurring physical maintenance visits. Consequently,      │
│  Internet of Things (IoT) diagnostic software functions as an incremental   │
│  operating expense rather than an immediate lever to reduce contractor      │
│  labor hours. Commercial property executives frequently defer these         │
│  digital retrofits when comprehensive modernization costs reach 3% to 6%    │
│  of total property value.                                                   │
│                                                                             │
│  ## Statutory compliance entrenches routine preventive maintenance over     │
│  digital substitution                                                       │
│  **Preventive maintenance commands 40.8% of global lift maintenance         │
│  expenditure because statutory frameworks, long-term service contracts,     │
│  and insurance rules mandate recurring manual inspections (Persistence      │
│  Market Research, indexed January 2026).** Although control systems are     │
│  the fastest-growing maintenance component due to microprocessors,          │
│  cloud-connected electronics, and remote software diagnostics, software     │
│  alerts cannot legally displace mandated physical site visits (Persistence  │
│  Market Research, January 2026). Baseline maintenance market projections    │
│  vary: Persistence Market Research valued the 2026 market at USD 41.7       │
│  billion, projecting USD 62.7 billion by 2033 at a 6.1% compound annual     │
│  growth rate (CAGR), whereas Fortune Business Insights valued the 2026      │
│  market at USD 38.33 billion, projecting USD 68.25 billion by 2034 at a     │
│  7.50% CAGR (Fortune Business Insights, indexed August 17, 2026). Under     │
│  either projection, the underlying spend remains locked into mandatory      │
│  manual service routines.                                                   │
│                                                                             │
│  ## Traction mechanisms absorb the majority of maintenance capital          │
│  **Traction systems account for 53.5% of total elevator maintenance spend   │
│  due to their dominance in high-rise commercial structures and rigorous     │
│  safety standards (Persistence Market Research, January 2026).** Within     │
│  the wider elevators market—which encompasses new equipment, maintenance,   │
│  and modernization, and is estimated at USD 86.3 billion in 2026 on track   │
│  to USD 136.9 billion by 2033—traction hardware held a 55.1% revenue share  │
│  in 2025 (Grand View Research, indexed July 2026). Furthermore, the Asia    │
│  Pacific region generated USD 15.86 billion in 2025, commanding 44.30% of   │
│  global maintenance revenues (Fortune Business Insights, August 17, 2026).  │
│  Because traction assemblies consume more than half of all maintenance      │
│  expenditures, predictive analytics initiatives that monitor secondary      │
│  components fail to yield measurable operational return on investment.      │
│                                                                             │
│  ## High modernization outlays force owners to defer proprietary OEM        │
│  diagnostic platforms                                                       │
│  **Major manufacturers are aggressively bundling predictive analytics into  │
│  proprietary ecosystems, yet high capital costs induce building owners to   │
│  defer installations (Fortune Business Insights, August 17, 2026;           │
│  Persistence Market Research, January 2026).** Otis Elevator Company has    │
│  deployed its Otis ONE remote diagnostic system across commercial           │
│  portfolios and major retrofits like the Burj Khalifa, while KONE           │
│  Corporation has rolled out its 24/7 Connected Services offering for        │
│  real-time analytics (Fortune Business Insights, August 17, 2026;           │
│  Persistence Market Research, January 2026). However, commercial property   │
│  owners routinely defer modernization when package costs exceed 3% to 6%    │
│  of property value, falling back on reactive maintenance instead of         │
│  incurring heavy upfront modernization outlays (Fortune Business Insights,  │
│  August 17, 2026; Persistence Market Research, January 2026).               │
│                                                                             │
│  ## What this means                                                         │
│  - **Budgetary implication:** Confidence in this assessment is Medium.      │
│  Decision-makers must budget predictive maintenance platforms as additive   │
│  software costs rather than anticipated labor reductions, focusing sensor   │
│  deployment strictly on traction components to address the 53.5% core cost  │
│  driver.                                                                    │
│  - **Ecosystem exposure:** Proprietary OEM offerings such as Otis ONE and   │
│  KONE 24/7 Connected Services create long-term vendor lock-in; commercial   │
│  asset managers must avoid capital outlays approaching 3% to 6% of asset    │
│  value without contractually guaranteed operational offsets.                │
│  - **Key uncertainties:** Confidence remains capped at Medium because       │
│  literature lacks third-party empirical validation proving exact            │
│  percentage drops in unplanned downtime, and regulatory agencies like OSHA  │
│  or BIS provide no published guidance permitting automated IoT diagnostics  │
│  to replace on-site physical inspections.                                   │
